        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What are the best men's running shoes for treadmill running?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most men, a light, comfortable neutral trainer such as the Saucony Ride 19. If you need a narrow or wide fit, the New Balance 880 v15 comes in men's narrow to 4E. For long easy runs, the ASICS Gel-Nimbus 28 has the most cushioning, and the ASICS GT-2000 15 suits men who prefer a stability shoe.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is the Inov-8 F-Fly good for treadmill running?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It is good for treadmill work inside a gym session: warm-ups, intervals and incline walking before or after lifting. It has a 4 mm drop and far less cushioning than a running shoe, so for continuous runs of 30 minutes or more most men will be more comfortable in a cushioned running shoe.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do men need different running shoes for a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not a separate category. Any good running shoe works on a treadmill. The belt does change priorities: breathability matters more because there is no airflow, outsole durability matters less, and fit matters more, because the same stride repeats for the whole session.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What are the best treadmill running shoes for men with wide feet?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Choose a shoe made in men's wide sizes rather than sizing up. The New Balance 880 v15 comes in men's 2E and 4E, and the ASICS GT-2000 15 in 2E and 4E in some colours. Standard men's width is D, so a 2E is one step wider.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Should I size up for treadmill running shoes?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Leave about a thumb's width between your longest toe and the end of the shoe, because feet swell during a warm indoor run. If the shoe is tight across the forefoot, try a wider version in the same length before going up half a size, which can leave the heel loose.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How often should men replace treadmill running shoes?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Around 300 to 500 miles is the usual guideline, judged by how the shoe feels rather than how the outsole looks, because the belt wears rubber very slowly. Heavier runners may get fewer miles from a pair. Replace shoes when the foam feels flat or familiar runs start to leave your legs sore.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

